{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the appearance of the plague, etc.</b> This matter reveals the sod of what is said (Psalms 39:7): ״אַךְ בְּצֶלֶם יִתְהַלֶּךְ אִישׁ״ — this is the appearance of living flesh. And when the aspect of evil gains dominion over a person, it will imprint upon human flesh the withdrawal of the life-giving luminosity (zoharat), and therefore the deficiency of appearance will be visible and it will be deeper than the skin — and therefore Scripture spoke in this manner: ״עָמֹק מִן הָעוֹר״. And according to the words of our Rabbis, may their memory be for blessing (Torat Kohanim ad loc.; Shavuot 5a), the reason Scripture says ״עָמֹק״ is to say that if all his flesh were the same appearance as the plague, he is pure — for no depth is discernible — and Scripture was particular about the depth." "<b>And the priest shall see it and declare it impure.</b> Scripture suspended the impurity upon the mouth of the priest, along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 119a), that two angels accompany a person on the eve of Shabbat to his home, etc., and the good angel answers against his will, etc. Now, since the priest is the one who atones for Israel from their impurity and from their afflictions, therefore HaShem directed that he should give his consent to this impurity that has gained its place in that man — until his return before HaShem, and then HaShem will purge his impurity from him."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:48.051792861Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}